{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"259c15b20aed068e0e68b1287f6dbfca51d64fa06c9284c98076c0da83072195","slug":"croakermodel3","location":"/subrepos/demography/Croaker/CroakerModel3.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"📖️ A speculative evolutionary scenario","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Daniel Grünbaum","given":"Daniel","family":"Grünbaum"},"name":"Daniel Grünbaum","affiliations":["School of Oceanography, University of Washington"],"id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"github":"https://github.com/organismal-systems/quant-org-bio","keywords":["models"],"copyright":"2026","affiliations":[{"id":"School of Oceanography, University of Washington","name":"School of Oceanography, University of Washington"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":3}},"source_url":"https://github.com/organismal-systems/quant-org-bio/blob/main/subrepos/demography/Croaker/CroakerModel3.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/organismal-systems/quant-org-bio/edit/main/subrepos/demography/Croaker/CroakerModel3.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"CroakerModel3.md","url":"/build/CroakerModel3-48a4bb1bc8983a797fbf1483af24c247.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"cite","url":"doi:10.1139/cjfas-57-10-2010","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Diamond ","key":"swb5y3AhAN"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"NzBEsnWu9P"}],"key":"BWHAdWCsKt"},{"type":"text","value":" (2000)","key":"VmYq62RUFC"}],"kind":"narrative","label":"Diamond_2000","identifier":"doi:10.1139/cjfas-57-10-2010","enumerator":"1","key":"pR4LG2x2t6"},{"type":"text","value":" carried out a detailed statistical analysis in which they estimated all the key parameters discussed so far.\nIn addition to reproducing their modeling approach and results, we can add a new dimension to predictions of croaker population trajectories by speculating about evolutionary changes through which croaker life history might adjust to increases or decreases in mortality.\nThis is of particular interest in the case of addition of significant fishing mortality to populations that evolved without this type of mortality.","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"nqn1gTy8O6"}],"key":"avV74IYhON"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In other fish species, a variety of life history changes have been observed in response to anthropogenic stresses.\nFor example, a species in which large adults are heavily fished might evolve towards allocating more resources to reproduction and fewer to growth in small adults.\nNote that this is a different phenomenon than a simple change in the relative frequency of different age or size classes, which might result directly from fishing mortality without any evolutionary changes.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"BiAlROAm7G"}],"key":"Z1eD5bLcMK"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An implication from ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"cKqWMALpEP"},{"type":"cite","url":"doi:10.1139/cjfas-57-10-2010","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Diamond ","key":"t7V6SaOXGo"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"Eh42UbcobB"}],"key":"cI8EDQj7yr"},{"type":"text","value":" (2000)","key":"OQVH93DOD1"}],"kind":"narrative","label":"Diamond_2000","identifier":"doi:10.1139/cjfas-57-10-2010","enumerator":"1","key":"zybdj6JqK4"},{"type":"text","value":" is that conditions had changed relative to historical patterns at the time of their study, in fishing-induced mortality and possibly in many other ways.\nThis is reason to speculate that croaker life history parameters might evolve away from current values, if those changes increase reproductive success.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"oFdR2oH5kf"}],"key":"amTn9yrYZu"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evolution of egg size","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"Wq1c641Jj1"}],"identifier":"evolution-of-egg-size","label":"Evolution of egg size","html_id":"evolution-of-egg-size","implicit":true,"key":"pttgnGTcTv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, we will consider changes in only one life history parameter: egg size.\nIf we assume that females have a specific amount of resources to invest in eggs, they face a trade-off between allocating those resources to more, smaller eggs or to fewer, larger eggs.\nThere is a large literature on the evolution of egg size.\nChanges in egg size have consequences for sperm-egg encounter rates, for movement through the water column, for predation rates, for development times, and many other factors in larval life history.\nHere, we will focus on one possible effect of a hypothetical change in egg size, which is the development time of early larval stages.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"FTseX4DHAu"}],"key":"SZ6V1A5FJx"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It’s important to keep in mind that we do not have data informing us about how development in croaker larvae actually takes place.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"dNwxyyRBU5"},{"type":"break","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"C5QFd41otM"},{"type":"text","value":"The discussion of egg size evolution is based on educated guesses, and is therefore highly speculative.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"sq0C45ujBX"}],"key":"ztasK2PxVS"}],"key":"sU46HTlxo6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will assume that an Ocean Larva must reach a critical size before it is able to transition into an Estuary Larva.\nTo do so, it must feed in the plankton.\nThis takes time during which it has a high probability of dying.\nIf a larva starts from a larger egg, it has less growing to do before it can transition.\nHence, its duration in the Ocean Larval stage is shorter, and its probability of surviving commensurately higher.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"guDKm6VtvK"}],"key":"LHcK9NC9Jc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this lab, we consider a scenario in which (over evolutionary time) females can adjust their egg size relative to the currently observed size by an ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"chhS4lp1j2"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Egg Investment Factor, ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"xCZh6VvSQi"},{"type":"inlineMath"